# Kit, described in Kit's own notation.
#
# kit:no-ui
#   Read by saturation.js, which excludes this corpus from the binding-saturation
#   study. That study asks whether the glue binding a spec to a UI saturates as
#   the UI grows; this corpus describes a command-line tool, so its nouns
#   (command:KitCheck, status:One) will never be bound to a locator and it has no
#   answer to give. The directive lives here rather than in a filename list
#   inside saturation.js so that it travels with the file that needs it.
#
# James asked (claude-code-bot#89): "think about how kit looks as a kit managed
# project". This corpus is the attempt, and the attempt is the measurement.
#
# ⚠️ READ THE NUMBER THIS PRODUCES CAREFULLY. `kit.js kit` will report ~0%
# generated, and that is NOT the same finding as james-habits-app's 0%. There,
# the app was missing nouns and Kit was working as designed. Here, every noun
# could be bound tomorrow and the number would not move, because the generator
# emits Playwright and Kit has no browser to point it at. The eight verbs it
# knows — opens, activates, sees, shows, attaches, lands, fills, state — are all
# browser verbs. A command-line tool has none of them.
#
# So this file is not a corpus that is waiting for bindings. It is evidence for
# a claim: **Kit can describe itself and can generate nothing for itself**, and
# the missing piece is not notation, it is a UI. That is the same UI James asked
# for in the first half of #89, which makes his two asks one ask.
#
# ── ✅ THAT PREDICTION HAS NOW BEEN TESTED, AND IT HELD ─────────────────────
# The UI shipped (#24 → #26 → #27 → #28) and `behaviours/kit-ui.beh` describes
# it. Kit derives **20 Playwright steps** for that corpus where this one still
# derives 0, and **5 of the 6 generated tests PASS against the running app**.
# So the number here is genuinely about the verbs and not about this file's
# authoring — the same author, same notation and same generator produce runnable
# tests the moment the subject has a browser.
#
# ⚠️ **THIS FILE'S 0 IS STILL THE FINDING AND MUST NOT BE "FIXED".** Do not add
# browser behaviours here to make the number move. The 0 is what makes the
# contrast measurable, and kit-ui.beh exists so that nobody has to.
# Full account: docs/pilots/kit-self-hosting.md.
#
# Every behaviour below is real, and every one is already tested in
# `kit.test.js` — the mapping in `kit.tests.json` names which test.

behaviour BEH-GATE-1 "A behaviour with no test naming it fails the build"
  actor engineer
  given corpus:SnipIt
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:One
  contract this is stage 0's stated deliverable in docs/timeline.md

behaviour BEH-GATE-2 "Could-not-look is never reported as green"
  actor engineer
  given repo:WithNoTests
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:Two
  contract exit 2 is distinct from 0 AND from 1 — a gate that reads nothing and
  contract exits 0 is indistinguishable in CI from a passing one

behaviour BEH-GATE-3 "A mapping naming a test that no longer exists is a hard failure"
  actor engineer
  given mapping:Stale
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:One
  contract this checkability is the whole argument for option C over markers

behaviour BEH-READ-1 "The reader refuses when its two counts disagree"
  actor engineer
  given file:CountsDisagree
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:Two
  contract the two counts must be different MECHANISMS — position versus lexical
  contract structure — or agreement between them proves nothing

behaviour BEH-GEN-1 "An unbound noun is refused, and the missing noun is named"
  actor engineer
  given corpus:UnboundNoun
  when runs command:KitGenerate
  then reports marker:Ungenerated
  contract no locator is ever guessed from the noun's own name

behaviour BEH-GEN-2 "An unsupplied route param is refused rather than truncated"
  actor engineer
  given corpus:RouteParam
  when runs command:KitGenerate
  then reports marker:Ungenerated
  contract emitting ./editor/ would navigate to the wrong page and look correct

behaviour BEH-PARSE-1 "An unrecognised keyword is an error, not silently dropped"
  actor engineer
  given corpus:BadKeyword
  when runs command:KitParse
  then raises error:UnrecognisedKeyword
  contract a spec language that ignores what it does not understand is how a
  contract spec becomes descriptive

behaviour BEH-RESOLVE-1 "A hole in one behaviour is filled by a different behaviour"
  actor engineer
  given corpus:CrossBehaviourHole
  when runs command:KitResolve
  then reports slot:Filled
  contract this is the part no BDD tool has, and it needs ONE symbol table

behaviour BEH-RESOLVE-2 "Two behaviours disagreeing is a conflict, with both sides named"
  actor engineer
  given corpus:Disagreement
  when runs command:KitResolve
  then reports conflict:BothSides

behaviour BEH-ADJ-1 "An inference is included by default and marked unreviewed"
  actor engineer
  given corpus:Inference
  when runs command:KitResolve
  then reports review:Unreviewed
  contract James's call on claude-code-bot#68 — default-include, marked

# ── the read API (docs/design/ui.md) ────────────────────────────────────────
# The first behaviours here that describe something with a NETWORK surface, and
# the first two are security properties. A security property asserted only in a
# comment above the code is a wish; described here it is named by a test, gated
# by check.js, and mutated by mutate.js.

behaviour BEH-UI-1 "A verb the server has no meaning for reaches no handler"
  actor engineer
  given request:Unsupported
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports status:MethodNotAllowed
  contract decision 2 landed on 2026-09-08, so POST now has a handler and this
  contract behaviour narrowed rather than disappeared — PUT, PATCH and DELETE have
  contract one meaning each that nobody has decided, and a route accepting a verb it
  contract cannot serve is a 405 waiting to be discovered as a 500

behaviour BEH-UI-2 "The server binds the loopback interface, not every interface"
  actor engineer
  given option:Default
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports host:Loopback
  contract 0.0.0.0 would publish every corpus in the working tree, and the absolute
  contract path of every repo beside it, to anything that can reach the host

behaviour BEH-UI-3 "A traversal in an app name is refused rather than resolved to a path"
  actor engineer
  given request:Traversal
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports status:NotFound
  contract the name is looked UP in the set of corpora that exist; it is never
  contract joined to a path, so there is nothing to traverse with

behaviour BEH-UI-4 "Coverage that could not be read is null in the list, never zero"
  actor engineer
  given repo:Absent
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports coverage:Unavailable
  contract project.js's rule, carried through the transport — a UI that cannot tell
  contract "no mapping exists" from "nothing is covered" renders the second

# ── the corpus writer (writer.js) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Decision 2 in docs/design/ui.md lapsed 2026-09-08 and its default became the
# decision: write the file, never touch git. These are the rules that decision
# rests on, and they are the first behaviours here describing something that
# CHANGES a file rather than reading one.

behaviour BEH-WRITE-1 "An edit splices one block, so every comment in the corpus survives"
  actor engineer
  source defined
  given corpus:WithComments
  when runs command:KitWrite
  then reports comment:Preserved
  contract a round-trip through parse() would delete every comment in the file, and
  contract snip-it.beh's nine opening lines are the caveat everything else rests on

behaviour BEH-WRITE-2 "An edit that would not parse never reaches the disk"
  actor engineer
  source defined
  given step:Malformed
  when runs command:KitWrite
  then raises error:WouldNotParse
  contract the corpus is the source of truth for everything else Kit says, so a
  contract writer that can corrupt it is worse than one that cannot write at all

behaviour BEH-WRITE-3 "An edit that would change a neighbouring behaviour is refused"
  actor engineer
  source defined
  given splice:OffByOne
  when runs command:KitWrite
  then raises error:CollateralChange
  contract a step landing in the next behaviour parses perfectly and is silently
  contract wrong — the worst failure a writer can have

behaviour BEH-WRITE-4 "The writer never touches git, and the file proves it rather than promising it"
  actor engineer
  source defined
  given decision:LapsedDefault
  when runs command:KitWrite
  then reports commit:None
  contract a bot that commits to a spec repo cannot be un-added after the first
  contract surprise, so the absence of child_process is asserted from source

behaviour BEH-WRITE-5 "A write route is unreachable unless the server is bound to loopback"
  actor engineer
  source defined
  given host:Routable
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports status:Forbidden
  contract decision 1 said local tool; combining reachable-from-the-network with
  contract edits-files-in-the-working-tree is an unauthenticated remote write, and
  contract it would arrive as a --host flag rather than as a decision of his

behaviour BEH-WRITE-6 "A hostile page gets no CORS header, so it cannot write through the browser"
  actor engineer
  source defined
  given origin:Hostile
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports header:Absent
  contract access-control-allow-origin: * was defensible while the server could
  contract not write; with a write route it means any page the developer has open
  contract can edit their working tree
